# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""General zebrafish knowledge: a handbook (always readable in the Fishipedia) and a
question bank on biology, methods, advantages and limits of the model, and ethics.

HANDBOOK entries are (topic, title, text).  BIOQUIZ entries are
(question, [correct, wrong, wrong], explanation shown after answering)."""

HANDBOOK = [
 ("Why zebrafish", "The case for the fish", "External fertilisation, transparent embryos, hundreds of eggs per pair each week, organs formed within days, small size and low cost, a sequenced genome with strong similarity to ours, and easy genetic manipulation: together these make the zebrafish a powerful vertebrate model."),
 ("Why zebrafish", "Watching life happen", "Because embryos develop outside the mother and are see-through, a single living animal can be imaged from one cell to a swimming larva. Fluorescent reporter lines let researchers follow individual cell types in real time."),
 ("Why zebrafish", "Numbers and statistics", "One pair can give 100-300 embryos in a morning. Large numbers make genetic screens, drug screens in 96-well plates and well-powered experiments possible at a fraction of the cost and space of mammals."),
 ("Limits of the model", "What a fish cannot tell you", "Zebrafish have no lungs, mammary glands, prostate or placenta, a two-chambered heart, and live at 28.5 C rather than 37 C. More than 400 million years separate fish and humans, so findings need confirming in other systems."),
 ("Limits of the model", "Two copies, twice the work", "The teleost genome duplication left many genes as two paralogs ('a' and 'b') that can share or split a function. Knocking out one copy may show little, and about 30% of human genes have no clear zebrafish counterpart."),
 ("Limits of the model", "Practical caveats", "Drugs added to the water are taken up to varying degrees, far fewer validated antibodies exist than for mouse, and laboratory strains are deliberately not fully inbred, so genetic background varies between fish and between labs."),
 ("Limits of the model", "One model among many", "Zebrafish complement rather than replace mice, cell culture, organoids and computer models. Good practice is to choose the simplest system that can answer the question, and to cross-check important results."),
 ("Body plan", "A vertebrate like us", "Zebrafish share the main organ systems of humans: brain and spinal cord, heart and vessels, blood, liver, pancreas, kidney, gut, bone, muscle, eyes and ears. Most human organs therefore have a fish counterpart to study."),
 ("Body plan", "Heart and circulation", "The heart has one atrium and one ventricle and pumps blood through the gills to the body. A larval heart beats about 120-180 times a minute - closer to a human rate than a mouse's 500-700 - which helps in studying heart rhythm."),
 ("Body plan", "Living without a heartbeat", "Early embryos are so small that oxygen reaches every cell by diffusion. They survive for days without circulation, so heart and vessel mutants that would die at once in a mouse can be studied in fish."),
 ("Body plan", "Blood and immunity", "Blood forms in the kidney marrow, not in bone. Red cells and thrombocytes keep their nucleus. Innate immunity works from the first days; adaptive immunity (T and B cells) matures only after several weeks."),
 ("Body plan", "Senses", "Zebrafish see in colour with four cone types (including UV), hear with inner ears containing otoliths, smell, taste, and feel water movement with the lateral line. Its hair cells are killed by the same drugs that damage human hearing."),
 ("Development", "From one cell to a larva", "Cleavage, blastula, gastrula (about 5-10 hours), segmentation (10-24 h, one pair of somites roughly every 30 minutes), pharyngula (24-48 h), hatching (48-72 h), then the larva. Ages are given as hpf and dpf: hours and days post fertilisation."),
 ("Development", "Mother's instructions", "For the first three hours the embryo runs on RNA and protein supplied by the mother. Its own genome switches on at the mid-blastula transition. 'Maternal-zygotic' mutants are needed to remove both sources of a gene product."),
 ("Development", "Life cycle", "Embryo to 3 days, larva to about a month, juvenile to about 3 months, then adult. Larvae live on yolk until about day 5, when the swim bladder has inflated and they must start to feed. Fish can live 3-5 years in the lab."),
 ("Development", "Becoming male or female", "Laboratory strains have no sex chromosomes. Sex is set by many genes together with the environment: crowding, heat or low oxygen give more males. Every juvenile first forms an ovary-like gonad, which then stays or turns into a testis."),
 ("Genetics", "Forward and reverse", "Forward genetics starts from a phenotype and hunts for the gene (random mutagenesis screens). Reverse genetics starts from a gene and asks what happens without it (morpholino knockdown, TILLING, CRISPR knockout)."),
 ("Genetics", "Mutant, morphant, crispant", "A mutant carries a heritable DNA change. A morphant is an embryo injected with a morpholino that blocks a gene's RNA for a few days. A crispant is an injected, mosaic F0 embryo edited by CRISPR. Each needs its own controls."),
 ("Genetics", "Making a stable line", "Cas9 and guide RNA, or a Tol2 transgene, are injected at the one-cell stage. The injected fish are mosaic founders; they are raised and outcrossed to find those passing the change to offspring. A clean line takes two generations - about six months."),
 ("Genetics", "Controls for knockdowns", "Because morpholinos can cause off-target effects, community guidelines (Stainier and colleagues, 2017) ask for comparison with a mutant, dose controls and rescue experiments before a phenotype is believed."),
 ("Genetics", "How genes are written", "Zebrafish gene symbols are lower-case italics (shha); the protein is written Shha. Human genes are upper case (SHH). Transgenic lines are named Tg(regulatory sequence:coding sequence), for example Tg(fli1:EGFP). ZFIN sets the rules."),
 ("Husbandry", "Water", "Fish are kept at about 28.5 C, near-neutral pH and on a 14 h light / 10 h dark cycle in recirculating systems. Bacteria in the biofilter turn toxic ammonia into nitrite and then into far less harmful nitrate."),
 ("Husbandry", "Breeding and raising", "Males and females are set up the evening before, often separated by a divider that is removed at lights-on. Eggs fall through a grid out of the parents' reach, and are raised in embryo medium (such as E3) in Petri dishes at 28.5 C."),
 ("Husbandry", "Food and health", "Larvae start on paramecia or rotifers, then brine shrimp (Artemia) and dry food. Facilities use quarantine, surface disinfection ('bleaching') of imported eggs, and sentinel fish to keep out pathogens such as Pseudoloma and Mycobacterium."),
 ("Husbandry", "Keeping lines safely", "Lines are archived as frozen sperm and revived by in-vitro fertilisation. This protects against loss, and means a line need not be kept alive when nobody is using it - which also reduces the number of animals bred."),
 ("Methods", "Seeing inside", "To keep larvae transparent, pigment can be blocked with PTU or removed genetically (nacre, casper). Larvae are anaesthetised with tricaine (MS-222) and mounted in low-melting agarose for confocal or light-sheet microscopy."),
 ("Methods", "Behaviour", "Larvae show robust behaviours from day 5: the startle response, following moving stripes (optomotor and optokinetic responses), light-dark activity changes and sleep. Hundreds can be tracked at once in multi-well plates."),
 ("Methods", "Drugs and toxicology", "Small molecules dissolved in the water are absorbed by embryos, so thousands of compounds can be tested on a whole animal. Zebrafish are also used to detect heart, liver and developmental toxicity, and in environmental testing (OECD test 236)."),
 ("Medicine", "Modelling disease", "Fish carrying mutations in human disease genes model muscular dystrophy (sapje, dystrophin), epilepsy (scn1lab, Dravet syndrome), heart muscle disease, anaemias, cancers such as melanoma and leukaemia, and infections such as tuberculosis."),
 ("Medicine", "Regeneration", "Zebrafish regrow heart muscle, fins, retina, spinal cord and hair cells. Understanding how they do it - mostly by making mature cells divide again - may show why mammals largely cannot, and how that might be changed."),
 ("Ethics", "The 3Rs", "Replacement, Reduction and Refinement were set out by William Russell and Rex Burch in 1959. Replace animals where possible, use the fewest that give a sound answer, and refine methods to minimise suffering. They underpin all animal-research law."),
 ("Ethics", "What the law protects", "In the EU (Directive 2010/63) zebrafish are protected from the stage of independent feeding, about 5 days after fertilisation. Experiments on protected animals need an ethically reviewed and authorised project, trained staff and an approved facility."),
 ("Ethics", "Can fish suffer?", "Fish have nociceptors and respond to harmful stimuli with changes in behaviour and physiology that painkillers reduce. How this is experienced is debated, so the precautionary approach is to assume they can suffer and to prevent it."),
 ("Ethics", "Refinement in practice", "Anaesthesia for procedures such as fin clipping, pain relief, less invasive genotyping (skin swabs, larval sampling), humane endpoints defined in advance, social housing for a shoaling species, sensible stocking density and enrichment."),
 ("Ethics", "Reduction in practice", "Plan sample sizes with statistics, image the same animal over time instead of using many, share lines and data (ZFIN, resource centres), breed only what is needed, and freeze lines instead of keeping them alive."),
 ("Ethics", "Replacement - and its limits", "Embryos younger than 5 days are often counted as partial replacement for older animals or mammals. But early larvae may already sense harm, so the same care applies: use the earliest stage that can answer the question."),
 ("Ethics", "Severity and humane endpoints", "EU procedures are classed as non-recovery, mild, moderate or severe. Harmful genetic lines count too. A humane endpoint is a sign, agreed in advance, at which an animal is taken out of a study and humanely killed to limit suffering."),
 ("Ethics", "Honest science is ethical science", "An experiment that cannot be trusted wastes animals. Randomise, blind the scoring, use several clutches, report every animal and negative results, and follow reporting standards such as the ARRIVE guidelines."),
 ("Ethics", "Genetically modified fish", "Transgenic and mutant lines must be contained so they cannot escape into the wild, recorded, and assessed for welfare. Regulations on genetically modified organisms apply in addition to animal-research law."),
]

